@startuml fig10_state_node
!include theme.iuml
title Figure 10 — State Machine: Worker Node and Its Capability Estimate

state "Offline" as OFF
state "Registering" as REG
state "Calibrating" as CAL
state "Serving" as SRV {
  state "Idle" as IDLE
  state "Batching" as BAT
  state "Saturated" as SAT
  [*] --> IDLE
  IDLE --> BAT : first request admitted
  BAT --> BAT : request joins / leaves\nthe running batch
  BAT --> IDLE : in-flight set empties
  BAT --> SAT : KV-cache pressure or\nqueue depth above threshold
  SAT --> BAT : pressure released
}
state "Draining" as DRN
state "Unreachable" as UNR

[*] --> OFF
OFF --> REG : process start
REG --> CAL : specs reported to scheduler
CAL --> SRV : cost model fitted\n(throughput x prompt length x concurrency)
CAL : short warmup benchmark;\nwithout it "hardware-aware" is guesswork
SRV --> DRN : operator drain\n(churn experiment)
DRN --> OFF : in-flight set empty
SRV --> UNR : heartbeat missed > k intervals
UNR --> SRV : heartbeat resumes
UNR --> OFF : declared lost;\nin-flight requests retried elsewhere

note right of SRV
  Observed throughput here is **non-stationary**.
  Its autocorrelation time tau and variance
  envelope are measured directly (MPR-1) and
  become the independent variable of H3 rather
  than an assumption to be swept under the rug.
end note

state "Estimate freshness at the scheduler" as EST {
  state "Fresh" as FRESH
  state "Ageing" as AGE
  state "Stale" as STALE
  [*] --> FRESH
  FRESH --> AGE : age approaches tau
  AGE --> STALE : age exceeds tau
  STALE --> FRESH : heartbeat received\nor staleness injection s reset
  AGE --> FRESH : heartbeat received
}

SRV -[#B45309,dashed]-> EST : heartbeat (F-10) resets age;\ninjected staleness s (F-8) holds it open

note bottom of EST
  This sub-state is scheduler-side, not node-side:
  it tracks what the scheduler *believes*, which is
  precisely the quantity H3 manipulates.
end note
@enduml
